Reply/forward subject and forwarding work, explored 2026-11-15 and set
aside for a dedicated "forwarding in a modern MIME world" project (see
revival/doc/roadmap.md).  All tested live except where noted.

- mimepart.c/.h, mimeparttest.c: mimepart_DecodeHeaderLossless (NULL
  when any character would become '?'), and mimepart_EncodeHeader
  (Latin-1 -> =?ISO-8859-1?Q?...?= words, 75-char words, 76-col folds).
  mimeparttest gains "lossless" and "encode" modes.
- reply.c: Re:/Fwd: subjects decoded when lossless, else kept raw.
  Set aside because lossy subjects (emoji, other scripts) still show
  raw, which is confusing.
- submsg.c: EncodeSubject encodes an 8-bit outgoing Subject on normal
  sends (not resends).  Worth keeping on its own: today a Subject typed
  with accents goes out as raw 8-bit.
- text822.c: message/rfc822 parts rendered inline (1994 behavior)
  instead of as "[attachment: ...]"; DISPLAY_KEY_HEADERS shows only
  Date/From/To/Cc/Subject of an enclosed message; in a draft, the
  enclosed message's HTML shown as plain text; plain-text mode prefers
  text/plain alternatives.  Without this, forwarding sends a one-line
  "[attachment: unnamed (message/rfc822, N bytes)]" in place of the
  forwarded message (a regression from the revival's multipart/mixed
  handling).
- htmltext.c: no "(url)" suffix when a link's text is its own URL, or a
  mailto: shown as the bare address.

Apply from the checkout root with: patch -p0 < patches/forwarding-mime-wip.patch

